  "slug": "intestinal-liver-mice-0dd6",
  "summary": "WGS-based T-cell infiltration scoring and a 244-sample CTCL atlas tie inferred tumor states to immunotherapy and outcome endpoints.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational inference of tumor and immune states from high-dimensional data, increasingly evaluated against clinical endpoints. CIDER estimates tumor T-cell infiltration from WGS-reconstructed CDR3 sequences, validates scores against RNA signatures, and predicts immunotherapy response. CATFISHR combines transcriptome similarity, inferred CNV profiles and consensus cell typing to identify malignant cells. A 244-sample scRNA-seq atlas in cutaneous T cell lymphoma applies pseudobulk mixed models to resolve microenvironmental changes and outcome-predictive molecular subtypes, while Multiplex-GCN integrates TME single-cell atlas-derived cell proportions with multi-omics to define seven pancreatic cancer subtypes described as clinically actionable. The scATAC and RNA multiomic study of CD4 and CD8 T cells in viral infection falls outside tumor profiling but shares the integrative single-cell framing. What is new relative to descriptive profiling is the explicit linkage of inferred states to outcome or actionable subtypes across WGS, single-cell and multi-omic layers.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-29"
}
